module.exports = [
  { q: ["When is the deadline to file my income tax return in Canada?", "whens the tax deadline", "tax filing due date?"], a: "For most people, the deadline to file your personal income tax return is April 30 of the year after the tax year. So for income earned last year, you file by April 30 this year. Any balance owing is also due on April 30, and interest starts after that. If April 30 falls on a weekend or a holiday, the CRA generally treats your return or payment as on time if it arrives by the next business day. Filing early also helps your refund and benefits arrive sooner.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What if April 30 lands on a Saturday or Sunday?", "deadline is on a weekend this year, do i get extra time", "Tax deadline falls on a Sunday — what now?"], a: "When April 30 falls on a weekend or a public holiday, the CRA generally considers your return filed on time and your payment made on time if it is received or postmarked by the next business day. That said, it's safer not to wait until the last minute: online systems can be busy, and banks may take a day or two to process payments. Check canada.ca each year for the confirmed dates.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I'm self-employed. When do I have to file my taxes?", "self employed tax deadline", "Do sole proprietors get until June to file?"], a: "Yes. If you or your spouse or common-law partner earned self-employment income (you ran a business as a sole proprietor or partner), the filing deadline is June 15. The important catch: any balance owing is still due April 30. If you pay after April 30, the CRA charges interest on the unpaid amount, even though your return itself isn't late until after June 15. A good habit is to estimate what you owe and pay it by April 30, then file by June 15.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["If I file on June 15 as self-employed, do I still pay interest?", "self employed - is payment due june 15 too", "freelancer here, when do i actually have to pay what i owe"], a: "Your payment is due April 30, even though your filing deadline is June 15. If you have a balance owing and pay it after April 30, the CRA charges interest starting May 1, compounded daily, until it's paid. To avoid that, estimate your tax owing and send a payment by April 30, then file by June 15. If you overpay, the extra comes back as a refund once your return is assessed.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["My husband is self-employed but I'm not. Is my deadline June 15 too?", "spouse self employed, do i get june 15 deadline", "Does the June 15 deadline apply to my common-law partner?"], a: "Yes. The June 15 filing deadline applies to you if you or your spouse or common-law partner carried on a business. So even if you only had employment income, you can file by June 15 when your partner is self-employed. But for both of you, any balance owing is still due by April 30, so interest applies to unpaid amounts after that date.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What happens if I file my taxes late?", "filed late what happens", "Is there a penalty for missing the tax deadline?"], a: "If you owe tax and file after the deadline, the CRA can charge a late-filing penalty, plus interest on the unpaid balance. Penalties can be higher if you've filed late before. If you're getting a refund or owe nothing, there's usually no penalty, but filing late can delay benefits like the GST/HST credit and Canada Child Benefit. The best move is to file as soon as you can, even if you can't pay everything right away. Current penalty and interest details are on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I can't afford to pay my tax bill. Should I still file?", "cant pay what i owe to cra", "owe taxes but have no money what do i do"], a: "Yes, file on time anyway. Filing on time avoids the late-filing penalty, which is separate from what you owe. Interest will still apply to the unpaid balance, so pay what you can by the deadline. If you can't pay it all, you can contact the CRA to ask about a payment arrangement, which lets you pay over time. You can also make payments through your bank or CRA My Account. If money is tight, a free tax clinic or a non-profit credit counsellor can help you plan.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I need to file a tax return if I had no income?", "no income do i still file taxes", "Should I file taxes even though I didn't work last year?"], a: "You may not be legally required to file if you owe no tax, but it's usually a good idea to file anyway. Many benefits and credits are based on your return, including the GST/HST credit, the Canada Child Benefit, and some provincial or territorial programs. Filing also builds RRSP contribution room if you had earned income, and keeps your records current. If your income is low and your situation is simple, a free tax clinic can file for you.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a T4 slip?", "whats a t4", "T4 meaning?"], a: "A T4 (Statement of Remuneration Paid) is the slip your employer gives you showing what you earned from a job during the year and the amounts deducted from your pay, such as income tax, CPP or QPP contributions, and EI premiums. You use it to report employment income on your tax return. Employers generally must issue T4s by the end of February. If you had more than one job, you'll get a T4 from each employer.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["My employer never gave me my T4. What can I do?", "missing t4", "didnt get my t4 slip yet its march"], a: "First, ask your employer or payroll department for a copy. Employers generally must issue T4s by the end of February, and many post them online. You can also check CRA My Account, where slips filed by your employer usually appear under tax information slips. If you still can't get it, use your final pay stub to estimate the income and deductions, attach a note explaining the situation, and file on time. Don't delay filing just because a slip is missing.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What's a T4A slip used for?", "got a t4a what is it", "Why did I receive a T4A instead of a T4?"], a: "A T4A reports various types of income that aren't regular employment wages. Common examples include pension or annuity income, some scholarships or bursaries, research grants, certain self-employed commissions, fees for services, and some death benefits. Because it covers many types of income, look at the box numbers to see what each amount is and where it goes on your return. If you received fees for services, you may need to report it as self-employment income.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a T5 slip?", "t5 slip meaning", "bank sent me a t5 why"], a: "A T5 (Statement of Investment Income) reports investment income such as interest, dividends from Canadian corporations, and certain foreign income, earned in a non-registered account. Banks and other financial institutions issue it when you earn investment income above a minimum reporting amount. Even if you don't receive a T5, you still must report all interest and investment income you earned. Income earned inside a TFSA isn't reported, and RRSP growth isn't taxed until you withdraw.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a T2202 form?", "t2202 tuition slip", "Where do I get my tuition receipt for taxes?"], a: "The T2202 (Tuition and Enrolment Certificate) is the slip your college or university issues showing eligible tuition fees you paid and the months you were enrolled. You use it to claim the tuition tax credit. Most schools make it available through your student portal, usually by the end of February. Keep it with your records. If you can't use all of your tuition credit, you may be able to carry it forward or transfer part of it to a family member.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a T4E slip?", "i was on EI got a t4e", "T4E what do I do with it?"], a: "A T4E shows the Employment Insurance (EI) benefits you received during the year and any income tax deducted from them. EI benefits are taxable income, so you must report them on your return. Service Canada issues the T4E, and you can usually view it in CRA My Account or your My Service Canada Account. If you had high net income for the year, you might have to repay part of your regular EI benefits, which is calculated on your return.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Why do I owe tax after being on EI?", "owe money because of employment insurance", "EI taxes owing why"], a: "EI benefits are taxable, but the tax withheld from them may not cover everything you owe, especially if you also worked during the year. When your income from all sources is combined, you may end up in a higher bracket than the withholding assumed. In some cases, people with higher net income also have to repay part of their regular EI benefits. To avoid surprises, you can ask Service Canada to withhold more tax from future EI payments.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What do I do with my RRSP contribution receipts?", "rrsp receipts where do they go", "got rrsp slips, how to claim"], a: "Your RRSP contribution receipts show how much you contributed and when. You report your contributions on your return (Schedule 7), and you can choose to deduct them that year or carry the deduction forward to a future year. Receipts for contributions made in the first 60 days of the year cover either the previous tax year or the current one. Check your RRSP deduction limit on your latest Notice of Assessment or in CRA My Account before contributing.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I lost my tax slips. How can I get them?", "lost my t4 and t5", "where can i find old tax slips"], a: "Sign in to CRA My Account. Under tax information slips, you can usually see T4, T4A, T5, T4E, RRSP and other slips that issuers have filed with the CRA. Many certified tax software products also have an Auto-fill my return feature that imports this information directly. Slips may not all appear until spring, so give it time. You can also ask the employer, bank, or school for a copy.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is NETFILE?", "netfile meaning", "how do i file my taxes online"], a: "NETFILE is the CRA's electronic service that lets you send your personal tax return directly to the CRA over the internet using certified tax software. You prepare your return in the software, then submit it through NETFILE. It's faster than paper, you get a confirmation number, and refunds typically arrive sooner, especially with direct deposit. The NETFILE service usually opens in late February each year. A list of certified software, including some free options, is on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What does certified tax software mean?", "is my tax app certified by cra", "Which tax programs can I use to file online?"], a: "Certified tax software is software the CRA has tested and approved for filing personal returns electronically through NETFILE. Some products are free, some free for simple returns, and some paid. The CRA publishes the list of certified products on canada.ca each year, so check there to confirm that the version you use is certified for the right tax year. I can't recommend a specific product, but the list notes which ones are free.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I still file my taxes on paper?", "paper tax return still allowed", "can i mail in my return"], a: "Yes, you can still file on paper. You can order a tax package from canada.ca or by phone, fill it in, and mail it to the tax centre listed for your area. Paper returns take longer to process, so refunds and benefits usually arrive later than with online filing. If you're not comfortable with software, a free tax clinic can file electronically for you if you qualify.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Are there free tax clinics in Canada?", "free tax help near me", "who can do my taxes for free"], a: "Yes. Through the Community Volunteer Income Tax Program (CVITP), community organizations host free tax clinics where volunteers file returns for eligible people. Clinics may be in person, virtual, or drop-off. In Quebec, the program runs jointly with Revenu Québec. They're meant for people with modest income and a simple tax situation. You can find a clinic near you by searching for free tax clinics on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Who qualifies for a free tax clinic?", "am i eligible for free tax clinic", "free tax clinic requirements"], a: "Free tax clinics through the Community Volunteer Income Tax Program are for people with a modest income and a simple tax situation. The income guidelines depend on family size and are updated over time, so check the current criteria on canada.ca. Clinics generally can't handle complex returns, such as people with self-employment expenses, rental income, capital gains or losses, bankruptcies, or deceased persons. If your return is complex, a CPA or accountant is a better fit.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is CRA My Account?", "what can i do in cra my account", "Is My Account worth signing up for?"], a: "CRA My Account is the CRA's secure online portal for individuals. You can:\n- view your Notices of Assessment and tax slips\n- check your refund status and balance owing\n- see your RRSP deduction limit and TFSA room\n- view benefit and credit payments\n- set up direct deposit and change your address\n- change a return and send messages to the CRA\nIt's one of the most useful tools for managing your taxes, and it's free.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I register for CRA My Account?", "sign up for my account cra", "how to make a cra account"], a: "You can register for CRA My Account on canada.ca. You can sign in with a CRA user ID and password, a Sign-In Partner (your online banking credentials with participating banks), or a provincial digital ID where available. Registration usually requires your social insurance number, date of birth, postal code, and an amount from one of your recent tax returns. You may get a security code by mail or be able to verify instantly.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I set up direct deposit with the CRA?", "direct deposit cra", "want my refund in my bank account"], a: "You can set up direct deposit in a few ways:\n- in CRA My Account, under your profile\n- through your bank's online banking, if it offers CRA direct deposit enrolment\n- by phone or by mailing the direct deposit form\nOnce it's set up, your refund and benefit payments, like the GST/HST credit and Canada Child Benefit, go straight to your bank account. It's faster and safer than cheques.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a Notice of Assessment?", "whats an NOA", "got a notice of assessment, what does it mean"], a: "A Notice of Assessment (NOA) is the statement the CRA sends after processing your return. It shows whether you get a refund, owe a balance, or are even, and lists any changes the CRA made to your return. It also shows useful info like your RRSP deduction limit for the next year. Read it carefully and keep it, since lenders often ask for NOAs when you apply for a mortgage or loan.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Where can I find my Notice of Assessment?", "how do i get my NOA", "lost my notice of assessment"], a: "Your Notice of Assessment is available in CRA My Account, where you can view and print it. If you registered for online mail, you'll get an email when a new notice is available. Otherwise, it's mailed to you. For online returns, it usually arrives within a couple of weeks of filing; paper returns take longer. You can also call the CRA to ask for a copy of an older notice.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a reassessment from the CRA?", "cra reassessed my return why", "got a notice of reassessment"], a: "A reassessment happens when the CRA changes your return after the original assessment. It might follow a review where they asked for receipts, a change you requested, or new information like a slip that wasn't on your return. Your Notice of Reassessment explains what changed and whether you now owe more or get money back. Generally, the CRA can reassess within three years from the date of your original Notice of Assessment, with longer periods in some situations.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I disagree with my tax assessment. What can I do?", "how do i dispute a cra decision", "cra got it wrong on my notice"], a: "First, contact the CRA to understand the change; sometimes it's a missing receipt you can simply send. If you still disagree, you can file a formal objection, usually online through CRA My Account or with the objection form. The deadline is generally 90 days after the date on your notice, or one year after your filing due date, whichever is later. For a complex dispute, it's worth talking to a CPA or tax professional.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I change my tax return after I've filed it?", "made a mistake on my taxes how to fix", "amend tax return canada"], a: "Wait until you receive your Notice of Assessment, then request a change. You can:\n- use Change my return in CRA My Account\n- use ReFILE through your certified tax software\n- mail an adjustment request form with supporting documents\nDon't file a second complete return for the same year, as that slows things down. Explain the change and keep your receipts handy in case the CRA asks.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I forgot to include a slip on my return. What now?", "forgot my t5 on my taxes", "missed a t4 when i filed"], a: "No panic: request a change to your return after you get your Notice of Assessment, using Change my return in CRA My Account or ReFILE in your tax software. Fixing it yourself is better than waiting, because the CRA often matches slips and may reassess you anyway, possibly with interest on any extra tax owing. Repeatedly failing to report income can lead to penalties, so it's worth correcting promptly.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a tax refund and a balance owing?", "why do i get a refund some years and owe other years", "refund vs owing explained"], a: "During the year, tax is usually withheld from your pay or benefits. When you file, your actual tax is calculated. If more was withheld than you owe, you get a refund. If less was withheld, you have a balance owing to pay by April 30. Common reasons for owing include having more than one job, self-employment income, investment income, or EI. Common reasons for refunds include RRSP contributions, tuition, childcare, or medical expenses.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How long does it take to get my tax refund?", "where is my refund", "when do i get my tax refund back"], a: "If you file online and have direct deposit, refunds often arrive within about two weeks. Paper returns take considerably longer. Delays can happen if the CRA selects your return for review or needs more information. You can track your refund status in CRA My Account. If you owe money to a government program, part of your refund may be applied to that debt first.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I pay my balance owing to the CRA?", "pay cra online", "ways to pay taxes owed"], a: "You have several options:\n- online banking, adding the CRA as a payee (often listed as federal taxes)\n- the CRA's My Payment service with a debit card\n- pre-authorized debit set up in CRA My Account\n- in person at your bank or at Canada Post with a remittance voucher\n- third-party payment services, which may charge fees\nPay by April 30 to avoid interest. Allow a few days for processing.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What are tax instalments?", "cra sent me instalment reminder", "do i need to pay taxes quarterly"], a: "Instalments are periodic payments toward next year's tax, for people who don't have enough tax withheld at source, like self-employed people, landlords, or investors. You may need to pay instalments if your net tax owing was above a certain threshold in the current year and either of the two previous years; the threshold is on canada.ca. The CRA sends instalment reminders with suggested amounts. Missing required instalments can lead to instalment interest.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["When are tax instalment payments due?", "instalment due dates", "quarterly tax payment dates canada"], a: "For most individuals, instalment payments are due March 15, June 15, September 15, and December 15. Farmers and fishers generally have a single instalment due December 31. If a due date falls on a weekend or holiday, the payment is generally considered on time if received the next business day. Your instalment reminder from the CRA shows the suggested amounts. An accountant can help if your income varies a lot.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I just moved to Canada. Why should I file a tax return?", "newcomer do i need to do taxes", "new immigrant first tax return why bother"], a: "Filing helps newcomers get money they may be entitled to. Your return is used to calculate benefits like the GST/HST credit and the Canada Child Benefit, plus some provincial or territorial benefits. Filing also starts building RRSP contribution room if you had earned income in Canada. Even with little or no Canadian income, filing is usually worth it. Free tax clinics welcome newcomers with simple situations.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How does my first tax return work as a newcomer?", "first year in canada taxes how", "what income do i report my first year as a resident"], a: "In the year you became a resident, you generally report your worldwide income from the date you became a resident onward, and you enter your date of entry on your return. Income earned before you arrived is generally not taxed in Canada, but it may still be asked for to calculate benefits. Each spouse or partner files their own return. Newcomer rules can get complicated with foreign income or property, so a free tax clinic or an accountant can help.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do newcomers apply for the Canada Child Benefit?", "new to canada how to get ccb", "apply for child benefit as immigrant"], a: "You can apply for the Canada Child Benefit as soon as you arrive and your child lives with you, rather than waiting to file a return. You can apply through CRA My Account or by submitting the CCB application form, plus a status-in-Canada form with information about your income from before you arrived. You'll then need to file a return every year to keep receiving payments. Details are on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I'm an international student. Do I have to file taxes?", "international student taxes canada", "do int'l students file a return"], a: "Many international students are considered residents of Canada for tax purposes and should file, especially if they worked or want benefits like the GST/HST credit. Filing also lets you claim your tuition credit, which you can carry forward to use when you have more income later. Your residency status depends on your ties to Canada, so if it's unclear, check the residency guidance on canada.ca. Many campuses host free tax clinics for students.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I got married. Do I need to tell the CRA?", "marital status change cra", "how to update marital status for taxes"], a: "Yes. If your marital status changes, such as marrying, becoming common-law, separating, divorcing, or being widowed, you should let the CRA know by the end of the month after the month your status changed. You can update it in CRA My Account. It matters because benefits like the GST/HST credit and Canada Child Benefit are based on family net income, so a change can affect your payments.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What does common-law mean for tax purposes?", "when are we considered common law", "living together count as common law for cra?"], a: "For federal tax purposes, you're generally considered common-law if you live with someone in a conjugal relationship and have done so continuously for at least 12 months, or you have a child together by birth or adoption, or one of you has custody and control of the other's child. Short separations of less than 90 days don't break the 12 months. Once you meet the definition, you report your partner's information and notify the CRA.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["We separated recently. How does that affect my taxes?", "separated taxes canada", "just split from my partner what changes"], a: "Let the CRA know about the change in CRA My Account so your benefits like the Canada Child Benefit and GST/HST credit can be recalculated based on your own income. Generally, you're considered separated once you've lived apart for at least 90 days due to a breakdown of the relationship. Support payments, custody arrangements, and claims for dependants all have specific rules. Since separation can be complex, a CPA or accountant, or a free tax clinic if your income is modest, can help.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I live in Quebec. Do I file two tax returns?", "quebec two tax returns why", "do quebecers file with revenu quebec and cra"], a: "Yes. Quebec residents file a federal return with the CRA and a separate provincial return (the TP-1) with Revenu Québec. In the rest of Canada, provincial tax is calculated on the federal return, but Quebec runs its own system. Both returns have the same deadlines: April 30, or June 15 if you or your spouse are self-employed, with any balance owing due April 30. Many certified software products prepare both at once.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What are RL slips in Quebec?", "got a relevé 1, what is it", "RL-1 vs T4"], a: "RL slips (relevés) are Quebec's version of tax slips, used for your provincial return with Revenu Québec. For example, if you work in Quebec, you get a T4 for your federal return and an RL-1 for your Quebec return. There are others, like RL-8 for post-secondary studies. The amounts can differ slightly between federal and Quebec slips, so use each for its own return.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Which province do I pay tax to if I moved during the year?", "moved provinces this year taxes", "Moved from Ontario to Alberta, which province on my return?"], a: "Generally, you pay provincial or territorial tax to the province or territory where you lived on December 31 of the tax year, for all your income that year (business income can have different rules). So if you moved from Ontario to Alberta in August, you'd usually file as an Alberta resident. If you moved into Quebec by December 31, you'd also file a Quebec return with Revenu Québec.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is an RRSP?", "rrsp explained", "registered retirement savings plan what is it"], a: "An RRSP (Registered Retirement Savings Plan) is a registered account to save for retirement. Contributions can be deducted from your taxable income, which can lower your tax for the year. Investments grow tax-deferred inside the account, and you pay tax when you withdraw, typically in retirement. Your contribution room is based on your earned income and is shown on your Notice of Assessment. Annual limits change each year; check CRA My Account or canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How does the RRSP deduction work?", "how much tax do i save with rrsp", "rrsp lowers taxes how"], a: "When you contribute to your RRSP, you can deduct that amount from your income, which reduces your taxable income. The tax saving depends on your marginal tax rate, so the same contribution saves more tax at higher incomes. You don't have to deduct contributions in the year you make them; you can carry the deduction forward to a year when your income is higher. You'll pay tax on the money when you withdraw it later.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the RRSP contribution deadline?", "last day for rrsp contributions", "rrsp deadline this year"], a: "You can generally deduct RRSP contributions made during the year, plus those made within the first 60 days of the next year. That usually means the deadline is March 1, or February 29 in a leap year. If the date falls on a weekend, the CRA may move it to the next business day; check canada.ca for the confirmed date each year. Contributions in that window can be claimed for the previous year or the current one.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How much can I put in my RRSP?", "rrsp limit", "whats my rrsp contribution room"], a: "Your RRSP deduction limit is personal: it's based on a percentage of your previous year's earned income, up to an annual maximum that changes each year, plus any unused room carried forward and minus pension adjustments. The easiest way to find your exact number is your latest Notice of Assessment or CRA My Account. Contributing more than your limit can lead to a penalty tax, so check before you contribute.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What happens if I over-contribute to my RRSP?", "put too much in rrsp", "rrsp over contribution penalty"], a: "If your contributions go beyond your RRSP deduction limit by more than a small allowed cushion, you'll generally owe a penalty tax on the excess for each month it stays in the plan, and you may need to file a special return. The fastest fix is usually to withdraw the excess. Check your exact room in CRA My Account. Because the rules here are technical, an accountant or CPA can help you sort it out.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I pay tax when I take money out of my RRSP?", "rrsp withdrawal tax", "cashing out rrsp early"], a: "Yes. RRSP withdrawals are added to your taxable income for the year, and your financial institution withholds some tax at the time of withdrawal. Depending on your income, you might owe more or get some back when you file. Also, the contribution room you used isn't restored when you withdraw. There are two programs that let you withdraw without immediate tax under conditions: the Home Buyers' Plan and the Lifelong Learning Plan, both repaid over time.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the Home Buyers' Plan?", "hbp rrsp house", "can i use my rrsp to buy a house"], a: "The Home Buyers' Plan (HBP) lets you withdraw money from your RRSP to buy or build a qualifying home without paying tax on the withdrawal at that time, generally if you're a first-time home buyer. You then repay the amount to your RRSP over a set number of years; any amount you don't repay as scheduled is added to your income. Withdrawal limits and repayment rules have changed over time, so check canada.ca for the current details.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a TFSA?", "tfsa explained", "tax free savings account how does it work"], a: "A TFSA (Tax-Free Savings Account) is a registered account where your savings and investment growth are tax-free. Contributions aren't deductible, but interest, dividends, and capital gains earned inside the account aren't taxed, and withdrawals are tax-free. Despite the name, a TFSA can hold more than savings, like GICs or investments. Your contribution room is personal and the annual amount changes; check CRA My Account or canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["When do I start getting TFSA contribution room?", "tfsa room starts when", "how is tfsa room calculated"], a: "You start accumulating TFSA contribution room from the year you turn 18, as long as you're a resident of Canada and have a valid social insurance number. Room accumulates each year whether or not you open an account, and unused room carries forward indefinitely. In provinces where the age of majority is 19, you can open a TFSA at 19, but the room from age 18 still counts. Your current room is shown in CRA My Account.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["If I withdraw from my TFSA, can I put it back right away?", "tfsa withdrawal recontribute", "took money out of tfsa when do i get room back"], a: "The amount you withdraw from your TFSA is added back to your contribution room at the start of the next calendar year, not right away. So if you withdraw in June and put it back in August of the same year without enough unused room, you could be over-contributing. Moving money directly between your own TFSAs at different institutions through a direct transfer doesn't count as a withdrawal.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What happens if I put too much in my TFSA?", "tfsa over contribution", "i overcontributed to my tfsa oops"], a: "If you contribute more than your available TFSA room, you'll generally owe a penalty tax on the highest excess amount for each month it stays in the account. The CRA may send you a letter and ask you to file a TFSA return. To stop the penalty from building, withdraw the excess as soon as possible. Your room is in CRA My Account, though it may not include very recent transactions, so keep your own records too.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What's the TFSA limit this year?", "tfsa contribution limit 2026", "how much can i put in tfsa this year"], a: "The annual TFSA dollar limit is set by the government and can change from year to year, so I don't want to give you a number that might be out of date. Also, your personal room includes unused room from past years plus withdrawals from previous years. The most reliable place to check your exact available room is CRA My Account, and the current annual limit is posted on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I pay tax on money I make in my TFSA?", "tfsa interest taxable?", "is tfsa income taxed"], a: "Generally, no. Interest, dividends, and capital gains earned inside a TFSA are not taxed, and withdrawals are tax-free. You don't report TFSA income on your return. A few exceptions exist: for example, the CRA may tax a TFSA used to carry on a business like frequent day trading, and some foreign dividends can have tax withheld by the other country. You also can't claim losses inside a TFSA.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is an FHSA?", "first home savings account explained", "fhsa how does it work"], a: "The FHSA (First Home Savings Account) is a registered account to help first-time home buyers save for a down payment. It combines features of an RRSP and TFSA: contributions are generally tax-deductible, and qualifying withdrawals to buy a first home are tax-free. There are annual and lifetime contribution limits, and the account can only stay open for a limited time. Exact limits and rules are on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I use both an FHSA and the Home Buyers' Plan?", "fhsa and hbp together", "can i combine fhsa with rrsp for a house"], a: "Yes. Under the current rules, you can make a qualifying FHSA withdrawal and a Home Buyers' Plan withdrawal from your RRSP for the same home, provided you meet the conditions of each. The FHSA withdrawal doesn't have to be repaid, while the HBP must be repaid to your RRSP over time. Eligibility rules are detailed, so confirm on canada.ca and consider talking to a licensed financial advisor about your situation.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is an RESP?", "resp explained", "registered education savings plan how it works"], a: "An RESP (Registered Education Savings Plan) is an account to save for a child's post-secondary education. Contributions aren't tax-deductible, but growth is tax-deferred. When the student withdraws the growth and grants for school, it's taxed in their hands, often at a low rate. The big draw is government grants like the Canada Education Savings Grant. In Quebec, there's also a provincial incentive.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the Canada Education Savings Grant?", "cesg resp grant", "does the government add money to resp"], a: "Yes. The Canada Education Savings Grant (CESG) is money the federal government adds to an RESP, based on a percentage of your contributions, up to annual and lifetime maximums. Lower-income families may get extra grant money, and eligible children may also get the Canada Learning Bond without needing to contribute. Quebec residents may also get the Quebec Education Savings Incentive. The child needs a social insurance number. Current grant rates are on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the Canada Child Benefit?", "ccb what is it", "child benefit how does it work"], a: "The Canada Child Benefit (CCB) is a tax-free monthly payment to eligible families to help with the cost of raising children under 18. The amount is based on your family net income, the number of children, and their ages, and it's recalculated each July. Both you and your spouse or common-law partner must file returns every year to keep receiving it. Amounts and thresholds change yearly, so check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Why did my Canada Child Benefit stop?", "ccb payments stopped", "didnt get my child benefit this month"], a: "The most common reason is that you or your spouse or common-law partner didn't file a tax return on time. Other reasons include a change in custody, marital status, income, or your address or bank info not being updated. Check CRA My Account for messages, file any missing returns, and update your information. If your income went up, your payment may have dropped or stopped. Contact the CRA if it still doesn't make sense.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the GST/HST credit?", "gst credit what is it", "why did i get a gst payment"], a: "The GST/HST credit is a tax-free quarterly payment to help individuals and families with low and modest incomes offset the sales tax they pay. It's calculated from your tax return and family net income, and is generally paid in July, October, January, and April. Amounts and income thresholds change each year. Filing your return on time, even with no income, is how you get it.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I apply for the GST/HST credit?", "apply gst credit", "do i need to apply for gst/hst credit"], a: "For most people, there's no separate application: the CRA automatically determines your eligibility when you file your tax return. You generally need to be at least 19, or have a spouse or common-law partner, or be a parent living with your child. Newcomers can apply in their first year using a special form for new residents. Keep filing every year, and keep your marital status and address up to date.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the Canada Workers Benefit?", "cwb explained", "canada workers benefit how to get it"], a: "The Canada Workers Benefit (CWB) is a refundable tax credit for low-income individuals and families who are working. It has a basic amount and a disability supplement. You claim it on your tax return, and the CRA may calculate it for you if you're eligible. Eligible people may also receive part of it as advance payments during the year. Income thresholds and amounts change yearly, so check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the disability tax credit?", "dtc explained", "disability tax credit what is it"], a: "The disability tax credit (DTC) is a non-refundable credit that reduces income tax for people with a severe and prolonged impairment in physical or mental functions, or the people who support them. Being approved can also open doors to other programs, such as the Registered Disability Savings Plan and certain disability supplements. If the person with the disability can't use the full credit, part may be transferable to a supporting family member.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I apply for the disability tax credit?", "dtc application", "how to get approved for disability tax credit"], a: "You apply with the DTC application form, which has a part you fill out and a part a medical practitioner must complete and certify. There's also a digital option where your practitioner can submit online. The CRA reviews it and tells you whether you're approved and for which years. If approved, you can ask the CRA to adjust past returns. Be cautious of companies that charge large fees to apply on your behalf; the application itself is free.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I claim medical expenses on my taxes?", "medical expense tax credit", "do medical bills reduce taxes"], a: "Yes, through the medical expense tax credit. You can claim eligible expenses you or your family paid that weren't reimbursed by insurance. The credit only applies to the amount above a threshold that depends on your income, and the threshold amount changes yearly. You can choose any 12-month period ending in the tax year. Families often combine expenses on one return, frequently the lower-income spouse's, to get past the threshold more easily.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What counts as an eligible medical expense?", "can i claim dental and glasses", "list of medical expenses for taxes"], a: "Many common costs can qualify, such as:\n- prescription drugs\n- dental work\n- prescription glasses and contact lenses\n- premiums for private health plans you pay yourself\n- some medical devices and some travel for medical care\nSome items need a prescription, and over-the-counter vitamins generally don't count. The CRA keeps a detailed list on canada.ca. Keep your receipts in case the CRA asks.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I deduct my moving expenses?", "moving expenses tax deduction", "moved for a new job can i claim the move"], a: "Possibly. You can generally deduct eligible moving expenses if you moved to start a job or business, or to study full-time at a post-secondary institution, and your new home is at least 40 km closer to your new work or school. You can usually only deduct them against income earned at the new location, and unused amounts can be carried forward. Expenses your employer reimbursed can't be claimed. Keep all receipts.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can students claim moving expenses?", "moved for university can i deduct", "student moving costs tax"], a: "Sometimes. If you moved to study full-time at a post-secondary school and your new home is at least 40 km closer to the school, you may be able to deduct eligible moving expenses, but only against the taxable portion of scholarships, bursaries, or research grants related to that study. Moving for a summer job can also qualify against that job's income. Many students don't have enough eligible income to use the deduction.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How does the tuition tax credit work?", "tuition credit explained", "do i get money back for tuition"], a: "The tuition tax credit is a non-refundable credit based on eligible tuition fees shown on your T2202. It reduces tax you owe, but it won't create a refund on its own if you have no tax to pay. If you can't use it all, you can carry the unused amount forward to future years when you have more income, or transfer part of the current year's amount to a parent, grandparent, or spouse, up to a limit.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I transfer my tuition credit to my parents?", "transfer tuition to mom", "give tuition amount to parent"], a: "Yes, you can transfer part of your current year's unused tuition amount to a parent or grandparent (or to your spouse or common-law partner), up to a maximum that's set in the rules. You first have to use what you need to bring your own tax to zero. You designate the transfer on your return and on your T2202. Amounts carried forward from previous years can't be transferred, only used by you.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I work from home. Can I claim home office expenses?", "work from home tax deduction", "home office deduction for employees"], a: "Possibly, if you're an employee and meet the conditions. Generally, you must have been required to work from home, worked there more than 50% of the time for at least four consecutive weeks or used the space exclusively to work and meet clients regularly, and your employer must complete form T2200. Then you can claim a portion of eligible expenses like utilities or rent. The temporary flat-rate method from the pandemic years is no longer available.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is form T2200?", "employer won't sign t2200", "do i need t2200 for home office"], a: "The T2200 (Declaration of Conditions of Employment) is a form your employer fills out and signs to confirm you were required to pay certain employment expenses, like home office costs. You need it to claim those expenses on your return, but you don't send it in; you keep it in case the CRA asks. If your employer won't sign it, generally you can't claim employment expenses. Commissioned employees may have additional options.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I need to report income from my side hustle?", "side gig income taxes", "do i pay tax on etsy sales"], a: "Yes. Income from a side business or gig, like freelancing, selling crafts, tutoring, or delivery apps, must be reported on your tax return, even if you don't receive a slip. You report it as business or self-employment income and can deduct reasonable expenses to earn it. Keep receipts and track income carefully. You may also need to register for GST/HST, and digital platforms now report seller income to the CRA. An accountant can help you set things up.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I drive for Uber. Do I need to charge GST/HST?", "rideshare driver gst registration", "uber driver taxes canada"], a: "Yes. Rideshare and taxi drivers must register for GST/HST, even if their income is small; the usual small supplier exemption doesn't apply to them. You also report your driving income as self-employment income and can deduct eligible expenses, like a portion of car costs based on business kilometres. Keep a mileage log. For other gig work, like food delivery, the rules can differ, so check canada.ca or ask a CPA.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I have to report cash tips?", "tips taxable canada", "do servers pay tax on tips"], a: "Yes. Tips and gratuities are taxable income, whether they're cash or paid electronically. Some tips are paid through payroll and appear on your T4, but tips received directly from customers must be reported by you. Keep a simple daily log of tips. Not reporting tips is a common reason for reassessments, so it's better to include them.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I pay tax when I sell my old stuff online?", "selling used things on marketplace taxes", "is selling my couch taxable"], a: "Usually not, if you're occasionally selling your own personal items like furniture or clothes for less than you paid. But if you buy items to resell for profit, or sell regularly like a business, that income is generally taxable and must be reported. The line depends on factors like frequency and intent. Digital platforms may report seller info to the CRA, so if you sell often, talk to an accountant.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Is cryptocurrency taxable in Canada?", "do i pay tax on crypto", "crypto gains taxes canada"], a: "Yes. When you sell, trade, spend, or give away crypto, it's generally a disposition and you must report any gain or loss. Depending on your activity, it's treated as a capital gain or as business income, for example if you trade frequently or mine as a business. Trading one crypto for another also counts as a disposition. Simply buying and holding isn't taxed until you dispose of it. For complex activity, talk to a CPA.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I claim crypto losses on my taxes?", "lost money on crypto tax", "crypto loss deduction"], a: "Often yes. If you sold or traded crypto at a loss, it's generally a capital loss that can offset capital gains in the same year, and unused capital losses can be carried back or forward. If your crypto activity is a business, losses are treated as business losses instead. Losses only count when you actually dispose of the crypto, not just when the price drops. Keep detailed records of every trade.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What records do I need to keep for crypto?", "crypto record keeping cra", "how to track crypto for taxes"], a: "Keep records of every transaction: dates, the type of crypto, the amount, the value in Canadian dollars at the time, what you received, fees paid, wallet addresses, and exchange records. You need this to calculate your cost and gains or losses. Exchanges can close or lose data, so download your history regularly. As with other tax records, keep them for at least six years.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I got a call from the CRA saying I'll be arrested. Is it real?", "cra calling says police coming", "Someone claiming to be CRA threatened me"], a: "That's a scam. The CRA never threatens you with arrest, jail, deportation, or police action. Hang up and don't give any personal or banking information. If you're worried that you really owe something, sign in to CRA My Account yourself or call the CRA using the number on canada.ca, not a number the caller gave you. You can report the scam to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["The CRA wants me to pay with gift cards. Is that legit?", "cra asking for bitcoin payment", "pay taxes with itunes cards?"], a: "No, that's definitely a scam. The CRA never asks for payment by gift cards, prepaid credit cards, or cryptocurrency like Bitcoin. Real CRA payments go through your bank, CRA My Account, or official payment options listed on canada.ca. Stop all contact, don't buy any cards, and report it to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. If you already paid, contact your bank and local police.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How can I tell if a message is really from the CRA?", "is this cra email real", "verify cra contact"], a: "Some signs it's a scam: threats, pressure to act right now, requests for gift cards or crypto, links asking for your banking or personal details, or refund offers by text or email. The CRA may contact you by phone, mail, or email notifications, but it won't send you links to collect personal information by text. When in doubt, don't click or reply. Sign in to CRA My Account directly to see if there's a real message.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I got a text saying I have a CRA refund waiting. Should I click it?", "text message cra refund link", "cra sms tax refund click here"], a: "Don't click it. Texts claiming you have a CRA refund or e-Transfer waiting are a common phishing scam designed to steal your banking info. The CRA does not send refunds through Interac e-Transfer or ask for personal information via text links. Delete the message. To check for a real refund, sign in to CRA My Account yourself. If you clicked and entered info, contact your bank right away.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How long should I keep my tax records?", "how many years keep receipts", "keep tax documents how long canada"], a: "Generally, you should keep your tax records and supporting documents for six years from the end of the tax year they relate to. That includes slips, receipts, and documents backing up any deductions or credits. Keep them longer in some situations, such as if you filed late or have an objection or appeal in progress. Digital copies are fine as long as they're readable.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What tax records should I keep?", "what receipts do i need to keep for taxes", "tax documents checklist"], a: "Keep anything that supports your return, such as:\n- tax slips (T4, T5, RL slips in Quebec, etc.)\n- receipts for credits and deductions: RRSP, donations, medical, childcare, tuition, moving\n- business income and expense records if self-employed\n- records of investment purchases and sales, including crypto\n- your Notices of Assessment\nThe CRA may ask to see them, so organize them by year and keep them for six years.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I contact the CRA?", "cra phone number", "talk to someone at cra"], a: "You have a few options:\n- sign in to CRA My Account to view info and send secure messages\n- call the CRA's individual enquiries line; the number and hours are on canada.ca\n- write to the CRA by mail\nBefore calling, have your social insurance number, your full name and date of birth, and info from a recent return ready to confirm your identity. Wait times are often shorter early in the day and outside tax season.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I authorize someone to deal with the CRA for me?", "give my accountant access to cra", "representative authorization cra"], a: "Yes. You can authorize a representative, such as a family member or accountant, to access your tax information and deal with the CRA on your behalf. You can do this in CRA My Account, or your representative can request access through the CRA's representative service and you confirm it, or by using the authorization form. You choose the level of access, and you can cancel it anytime.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I file taxes for my elderly mom?", "help my parent with her taxes", "doing my dads taxes for him"], a: "You can help her prepare and file her return, but she's still responsible for it and must agree to what's filed. To speak with the CRA for her or view her info online, she'll need to authorize you as her representative. If she has a modest income and simple situation, a free tax clinic is another option. If you're managing her finances under a power of attorney, the CRA may need a copy.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How can I hide my cash income from the CRA?", "how not to report cash jobs", "can i skip reporting cash payments, nobody will know"], a: "I can't help with hiding income; not reporting it is tax evasion and can lead to reassessments, penalties, interest, and even prosecution. The honest route is often less painful than people expect: report your income and claim every legitimate expense and credit you're entitled to. If you didn't report income in past years, the CRA has a Voluntary Disclosures Program that may reduce penalties. A CPA or accountant can help you sort it out properly.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can you make me some donation receipts for my taxes?", "need fake receipts for deductions", "how to create medical receipts to claim"], a: "I can't help create fake receipts. Claiming deductions with false documents is tax fraud and can lead to penalties or charges. What I can do is help you find legitimate deductions and credits you may be missing, like medical expenses for the whole family, charitable donations from registered charities, or tuition carry-forwards. If your situation is complicated, an accountant or a free tax clinic can make sure you claim everything you're entitled to.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can you help me make a T4 to show the bank for a loan?", "need a fake t4 for mortgage", "edit my notice of assessment to show higher income"], a: "I can't help with that. Creating or altering a T4 or Notice of Assessment is document fraud, and lenders often verify income directly. What I can help with is the honest route: understanding which documents lenders accept, how self-employed people can prove income, or how to improve your application by lowering debt or building credit. A mortgage professional can explain what options fit your real income.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["I haven't filed my taxes for a few years. What should I do?", "behind on taxes several years", "never filed taxes how to catch up"], a: "Start filing the missing years as soon as you can. If you're owed refunds or benefits, you might get money back. If you owe, filing sooner limits further interest and penalties. Gather your slips through CRA My Account, then file each year using certified software or on paper. If there's unreported income, ask about the CRA's Voluntary Disclosures Program. A free tax clinic or an accountant can help you catch up.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I pay tax when I sell my house?", "selling my home capital gains", "principal residence exemption explained"], a: "If the home was your principal residence for every year you owned it, the gain is usually fully tax-free under the principal residence exemption. You still need to report the sale and the principal residence designation on your return for the year you sell. If it wasn't your principal residence for all those years, such as a rental property or cottage, part of the gain may be taxable. For mixed situations, a CPA is helpful.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I claim charitable donations on my taxes?", "donation tax credit", "do donations reduce my taxes"], a: "Yes. Donations to registered Canadian charities and other qualified donees can give you a non-refundable tax credit. You need an official donation receipt with the charity's registration number. The credit rate is higher on the portion of donations above a certain amount, so spouses or common-law partners often combine their receipts on one return. You can also carry unused donations forward for a few years. You can check whether a charity is registered on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Can I deduct daycare costs?", "child care expenses deduction", "claim daycare on taxes canada"], a: "Often yes. Eligible child care expenses, like daycare, babysitters, day camps, and some boarding schools, can be deducted if they let you work, run a business, or go to school. Generally, the spouse or partner with the lower net income must make the claim, with a few exceptions. There are maximum amounts per child that depend on age and disability. Keep receipts with the provider's name and, for individuals, their social insurance number.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a refundable and non-refundable tax credit?", "refundable vs non refundable credit", "non-refundable credit meaning"], a: "A non-refundable credit can reduce the tax you owe down to zero, but it won't give you money back beyond that. Examples include the tuition, medical expense, and donation credits. A refundable credit can be paid to you even if you owe no tax. Examples include the Canada Workers Benefit and the GST/HST credit. That's why some low-income people get money back even when they owe no tax.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is the difference between a tax deduction and a tax credit?", "deduction vs credit", "is a deduction better than a credit"], a: "A deduction reduces your taxable income before tax is calculated, so its value depends on your marginal tax rate. Examples are RRSP contributions, child care expenses, and moving expenses. A credit reduces your tax directly, usually at a set rate. Examples are the tuition, medical, and donation credits. Neither is automatically better; it depends on your income and the specific item.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How does the tax bracket system work in Canada?", "if i earn more will all my income be taxed higher", "marginal tax rate explained"], a: "Canada uses a progressive system: income is taxed in layers called brackets, and each layer has its own rate. Moving into a higher bracket only means the dollars above that threshold are taxed at the higher rate, not all your income. So a raise never makes you take home less from that raise because of brackets. You pay both federal and provincial or territorial tax. Rates and brackets change yearly; check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Do I need a SIN to file my taxes?", "no social insurance number how to file", "can i file taxes without a sin"], a: "You generally need a social insurance number (SIN) to file a tax return in Canada; you get one from Service Canada. If you're not eligible for a SIN but need to file or apply for benefits, you can apply to the CRA for a tax number instead. Keep your SIN private and only give it when legally required, like to your employer, bank for interest-earning accounts, or government programs.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is Auto-fill my return?", "can my tax software pull my slips automatically", "autofill cra"], a: "Auto-fill my return is a CRA service that lets certified tax software download information the CRA has about you, like your tax slips, RRSP contribution info, and carry-forward amounts, directly into your return. You need to be registered for CRA My Account to use it. It saves time and reduces errors, but you should still review everything and add any income or deductions the CRA doesn't have, like self-employment income.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Why do I owe tax when I have two jobs?", "two jobs owe taxes", "second job tax owing why"], a: "Each employer withholds tax as if their job were your only income. So each one applies the basic personal amount and lower brackets, and combined, not enough tax is withheld. When you file, your total income is taxed properly and you owe the difference. To avoid a surprise, you can ask one employer to deduct extra tax, by updating your TD1 form, or set money aside during the year.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["What is a TD1 form?", "new job asked me to fill td1", "td1 personal tax credits return"], a: "The TD1 (Personal Tax Credits Return) is the form you give your employer or pension payer so they know how much tax to deduct from your pay. It lists credits you expect to claim, like the basic personal amount. There's a federal TD1 and, outside Quebec, a provincial one; in Quebec, there's a separate Revenu Québec form. If you have more than one job, you usually claim the credits with only one employer, and you can request extra tax deducted.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Should I do my taxes myself or hire an accountant?", "is it worth paying someone to do my taxes", "diy taxes vs accountant"], a: "It depends on your situation. Doing it yourself with certified software often works well for simple returns, like employment income, a few slips, and common credits. An accountant can be worth it if you're self-employed, have rental or foreign income, sold property or crypto, went through a separation, or are dealing with a death in the family. If your income is modest and your return is simple, a free tax clinic is another option. A CPA can tell you whether your case needs one.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Should I claim my RRSP deduction this year or save it for later?", "carry forward rrsp deduction worth it", "should i deduct rrsp now or next year"], a: "Some general considerations: the deduction saves more tax in a year when your income is higher, so people sometimes carry it forward if they expect a big income increase soon. On the other hand, deducting now gives you the tax benefit right away and keeps things simple. I can't give personal tax advice on what's best for you. A CPA or accountant can run the numbers for your situation.", lang: "en", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["How do I open a bank account as a newcomer to Canada?", "new to canada need bank account", "open bank account immigrant"], a: "Most banks and credit unions have newcomer packages. Generally, you'll need:\n- valid identification, such as your passport\n- your immigration document, like a permanent resident card, confirmation of permanent residence, or a study or work permit\n- sometimes proof of address\nYou don't always need a job or a SIN to open a basic account. Compare fees and features, book an appointment, and ask what ID they accept. Some banks let you start the process before you arrive.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Can a bank refuse to open an account for me?", "bank said no to opening account", "do i need a job to open a bank account"], a: "Under federal rules, banks must generally let you open a personal account even if you don't have a job, don't make an initial deposit, or have been bankrupt, as long as you provide acceptable identification. They can refuse in limited cases, for example if they suspect fraud or you give false information. If you're refused, ask for the reason in writing and use the bank's complaint process. The Financial Consumer Agency of Canada explains your rights.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What is a credit score in Canada?", "how do credit scores work", "credit score range canada"], a: "A credit score is a number that shows lenders how likely you are to repay borrowed money. In Canada, scores generally range from 300 to 900, and higher is better. They're calculated by the two main credit bureaus, Equifax and TransUnion, based on your credit report: payment history, how much of your available credit you use, how long you've had credit, new applications, and the types of credit you have.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["How can I check my credit score for free?", "free credit report canada", "check my credit equifax transunion"], a: "You can request your credit report for free from both Equifax and TransUnion, and both offer free online access through their websites. Many banks and some financial apps also show a free credit score. Checking your own report or score is a soft check and doesn't hurt your credit. It's a good idea to review both reports regularly for errors or signs of identity theft.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["How do I build credit as a newcomer?", "no credit history in canada how to start", "build credit from zero"], a: "Common ways to start:\n- get a credit card, such as a newcomer card or a secured card\n- use it for small purchases and pay the full balance on time every month\n- keep your balance well below your limit\n- pay all bills on time, including phone bills\n- avoid applying for lots of credit at once\nBuilding a good score takes time, usually months to years. Keep your oldest accounts open to lengthen your credit history.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What is a secured credit card?", "secured card how does it work", "is a secured credit card good for building credit"], a: "A secured credit card requires you to give the card issuer a deposit, which usually becomes your credit limit. You use it like a normal credit card, and as long as the issuer reports to the credit bureaus, on-time payments help build your credit history. It's often used by newcomers or people rebuilding credit. Confirm that the card reports to Equifax and TransUnion, and check its fees before applying.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What hurts my credit score?", "why did my credit score drop", "things that lower credit score"], a: "Common reasons a score drops:\n- late or missed payments\n- using a large share of your credit limits\n- many credit applications in a short time\n- accounts sent to collections\n- closing old accounts, which can shorten your history\n- bankruptcy or a consumer proposal\nIf the drop seems wrong, check your reports from both Equifax and TransUnion for errors or accounts you don't recognize.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a chequing and savings account?", "chequing vs savings", "which account do i need chequing or savings"], a: "A chequing account is for everyday money: paying bills, debit purchases, e-Transfers, and direct deposits. It often has unlimited or many transactions but pays little or no interest. A savings account is for money you want to set aside; it usually pays more interest but may limit or charge for withdrawals. Many people use both: chequing for spending, savings for goals and an emergency fund.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["How does Interac e-Transfer work?", "how to send an etransfer", "etransfer basics"], a: "Interac e-Transfer lets you send money from your bank account to someone using just their email or mobile number. Through online or mobile banking, you choose the recipient and amount. If they have Autodeposit set up, the money goes straight into their account. Otherwise, they get a notification and answer a security question to deposit it. Transfers are usually fast, but once deposited, they generally can't be reversed, so double-check details.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What is e-Transfer Autodeposit?", "autodeposit should i turn it on", "etransfer autodeposit meaning"], a: "Autodeposit lets e-Transfers sent to your registered email or phone number go straight into your bank account, without a security question. It's more secure than security questions, which can be guessed or intercepted, and it's faster. You set it up in your bank's online or mobile banking. When sending money, a recipient with Autodeposit will show their registered name, so you can confirm you're sending to the right person.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What are common e-Transfer scams?", "etransfer scam", "someone sent me too much by etransfer"], a: "Watch out for:\n- fake e-Transfer notification emails or texts with links that steal your banking login\n- overpayment scams, where someone sends too much and asks you to send the difference back\n- online sellers who want payment before you see the item, then disappear\n- fake landlords asking for a deposit before a viewing\nOnly accept transfers through your actual banking app, and remember e-Transfers are like cash.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["I sent an e-Transfer to the wrong person. Can I get it back?", "etransfer sent to wrong email", "cancel an etransfer"], a: "If it hasn't been deposited yet, you may be able to cancel it in your online banking. If it was already deposited, especially with Autodeposit, it usually can't be reversed by your bank. Contact your bank right away; they may try to contact the recipient's bank, but recovery isn't guaranteed. If it was fraud, also report it to the police and the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. Always confirm the recipient's details before sending.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What is CDIC insurance?", "is my money safe if my bank fails", "cdic deposit insurance explained"], a: "The Canada Deposit Insurance Corporation (CDIC) protects eligible deposits at its member institutions, such as most banks, if the institution fails. Coverage applies to eligible deposits like savings and chequing accounts and GICs, up to a set limit per depositor in each insured category, such as accounts in your name, joint accounts, TFSAs, and RRSPs. It's free and automatic. Stocks, mutual funds, and crypto aren't covered. Check the current limits on the CDIC's site.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Are credit union deposits insured?", "is desjardins covered by cdic", "credit union deposit protection"], a: "Usually yes, but not always by the CDIC. Most credit unions are provincially regulated, so their deposits are protected by a provincial deposit insurance program instead, with rules and limits that vary by province. In Quebec, deposits at Desjardins caisses are protected through the Autorité des marchés financiers. Ask your credit union which program covers your deposits and what's included.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["How does a mortgage work?", "mortgage basics canada", "what is a mortgage term vs amortization"], a: "A mortgage is a loan to buy a home, with the home as security. Key ideas:\n- amortization: the total time to pay off the loan\n- term: the period your current interest rate and conditions apply, after which you renew\n- down payment: what you pay upfront\n- payment frequency: monthly, biweekly, etc.\nYou'll also go through a qualifying process that includes a stress test. A licensed mortgage professional can walk you through options.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a fixed and variable mortgage?", "fixed vs variable rate mortgage", "variable mortgage meaning"], a: "With a fixed-rate mortgage, your interest rate stays the same for the term, so payments are predictable. With a variable-rate mortgage, the rate moves with the lender's prime rate, so your interest costs can go up or down; depending on the product, your payment or the share going to interest changes. Fixed offers stability; variable can cost less or more depending on rates. Which suits you depends on your budget and risk comfort.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What is the mortgage stress test?", "stress test canada mortgage", "why do i need to qualify at a higher rate"], a: "The mortgage stress test requires you to prove you could still afford your payments at a qualifying rate that's higher than the rate you'll actually pay. It's meant to make sure borrowers can handle rate increases. It applies to many mortgages from federally regulated lenders. The qualifying rate rules are set by regulators and can change, so ask your lender or a mortgage professional for current details.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What does amortization mean?", "amortization period explained", "25 or 30 year amortization difference"], a: "Amortization is the total length of time it would take to pay off your mortgage completely, if conditions stayed the same. A longer amortization lowers each payment but means you pay more interest overall. A shorter one raises payments but saves interest. The amortization is different from the term, which is how long your current contract and rate last. Maximum allowed amortizations depend on the type of mortgage and current rules.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Do I need 20% down to buy a house?", "down payment minimum canada", "what is mortgage default insurance"], a: "No, you can often buy with less than 20% down, but then you'll generally need mortgage default insurance, which protects the lender if you can't pay. The insurance premium is usually added to your mortgage. Minimum down payments depend on the purchase price, and the rules change over time, so check with a lender. A 20% or larger down payment generally avoids the insurance cost.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between a line of credit and a credit card?", "loc vs credit card", "line of credit explained"], a: "Both let you borrow up to a limit and repay over time. A line of credit usually has a lower interest rate, charges interest from the day you borrow, and is often used for larger or planned costs. A credit card usually has a higher rate but offers an interest-free grace period on purchases if you pay the full balance by the due date. Secured lines of credit, like a home equity line, use an asset as security.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["How do credit card interest charges work?", "how to avoid credit card interest", "why am i paying interest on my credit card"], a: "Most credit cards give you a grace period on new purchases: if you pay your full statement balance by the due date, you pay no interest on them. If you pay only part, interest generally applies to purchases, and cash advances usually start charging interest immediately. Paying only the minimum can keep you in debt for years. Credit card interest rates are high, so paying in full each month is the best habit.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["How do I make a budget?", "budgeting basics", "help me start a budget"], a: "A simple way to start:\n1. List your monthly take-home income.\n2. Track your spending for a month to see where money goes.\n3. Split costs into needs (rent, groceries, transit), wants, and savings or debt payments.\n4. Set limits for each category and adjust as needed.\n5. Automate savings on payday.\nUse an app, spreadsheet, or notebook, whatever you'll stick with. Review it monthly.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What is the 50/30/20 rule?", "50 30 20 budget", "is 50/30/20 a good budget rule"], a: "The 50/30/20 rule is a simple budgeting guideline: about 50% of take-home pay for needs, 30% for wants, and 20% for savings and debt repayment. It's a starting point, not a strict rule. In high-cost cities, needs like rent can take more than half, so many people adjust the percentages. The main idea is to give every dollar a purpose and save consistently.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What is an emergency fund?", "how much should my emergency fund be", "do i need an emergency fund"], a: "An emergency fund is money set aside for unexpected costs, like a job loss, car repair, or urgent travel, so you don't have to rely on credit. A common guideline is three to six months of essential expenses, but even a small amount helps. Keep it somewhere easy to access and separate from everyday spending, like a savings account. Start small and add to it automatically each payday.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Where should I keep my emergency fund?", "best place for emergency savings", "emergency fund in tfsa or savings"], a: "Generally, an emergency fund should be safe and easy to access quickly, so people often keep it in a high-interest savings account, sometimes inside a TFSA to keep the interest tax-free. Investments that can drop in value aren't usually ideal for money you might need suddenly. I can't recommend a specific bank or product. A licensed financial advisor can help you decide what fits your situation.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Should I buy bitcoin?", "is crypto a good investment", "should i put my savings in crypto"], a: "Some general points: crypto prices can swing a lot, it's not covered by CDIC deposit insurance, platforms can fail or be hacked, and scams are common. Gains are also taxable in Canada. Some people hold a small amount as part of a larger plan; others avoid it entirely. I can't give personal financial advice or tell you whether to buy any asset. A licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner can look at your goals and risk tolerance.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Which stock will go up next?", "give me a stock pick", "what stock should i buy right now"], a: "I can't predict stock prices or recommend specific stocks, and nobody can reliably know which stock will go up. In general, people consider their goals, timeline, risk tolerance, diversification, and fees when investing. If you want help building an investment plan, a licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner can give advice based on your full situation. I'm happy to explain investing terms or how accounts like TFSAs and RRSPs work.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Should I put my money in a TFSA or an RRSP?", "tfsa or rrsp which is better for me", "should i do rrsp or tfsa first"], a: "General considerations: an RRSP gives a deduction now and you pay tax on withdrawals later, so it tends to help more when your income today is higher than you expect in retirement. A TFSA gives no deduction but withdrawals are tax-free and flexible, which can suit lower incomes or shorter-term goals. Many people use both. I can't give personal financial advice. A licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner can help, and a CPA for the tax side.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Should I pay off debt or invest?", "pay down debt vs invest", "should i pay my loan first or put money in tfsa"], a: "Some general considerations: high-interest debt like credit cards often costs more than investments reliably earn, so many people prioritize paying it off. With low-interest debt, the choice is less clear and depends on your goals, risk comfort, and whether you have an emergency fund. Employer matching programs can also be a factor. I can't give personal financial advice. A licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner can look at your full picture.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What's the best bank in Canada for me?", "which bank should i choose", "best bank for newcomers"], a: "I can't recommend a specific bank, but here's what to compare:\n- monthly fees and what they include\n- number of transactions allowed\n- branch and ATM access where you live and work\n- online and mobile banking features\n- newcomer or student offers, and when they end\n- deposit insurance coverage\nIf you want advice tailored to your finances, a licensed financial advisor can help.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Which credit card should I get?", "best credit card canada", "recommend me a credit card"], a: "I can't recommend a specific card, but useful things to compare are: the annual fee, interest rates on purchases and cash advances, rewards and whether they match how you spend, the income or credit requirements, and extra features like insurance. If you're building credit, a no-fee or secured card is a common starting point. Most importantly, choose a card you can pay in full each month. A licensed financial advisor can help with personal advice.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Should I get a fixed or variable mortgage?", "fixed or variable which is better for me right now", "should i lock in my mortgage rate"], a: "General considerations: fixed rates give predictable payments, which helps if your budget is tight or you prefer certainty. Variable rates can save or cost money depending on how rates move, so they suit people who can handle payment or interest changes. Your term length, prepayment plans, and penalties for breaking the mortgage also matter. I can't give personal financial advice. A licensed mortgage professional or financial advisor can compare options for you.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Should I buy a house or keep renting?", "rent vs buy canada", "is it better to buy a home now"], a: "Some general considerations: buying builds equity but comes with a down payment, closing costs, property taxes, maintenance, and less flexibility. Renting offers flexibility and fewer surprise costs, and the money you would have put into a home can be saved elsewhere. Your timeline, job stability, local prices, and savings all matter. I can't give personal financial advice. A licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner can help you compare.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What should I invest in?", "where should i invest my money", "good investments for beginners?"], a: "I can't recommend specific investments, but I can share general ideas people consider: your goals and timeline, how much risk you can handle, keeping an emergency fund first, diversification, fees, and using registered accounts like a TFSA, RRSP, or FHSA where they fit. Check that anyone selling investments is registered with a securities regulator. For a personal plan, talk to a licensed financial advisor or certified financial planner.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["I'm drowning in debt. Where can I get help?", "too much debt help canada", "cant pay my credit cards anymore"], a: "You're not alone, and there are real options. Start by listing all debts, interest rates, and minimum payments. A non-profit credit counselling agency can review your budget and may offer a debt management plan. For serious debt, a Licensed Insolvency Trustee can explain options like a consumer proposal or bankruptcy; first consultations are typically free. Be cautious of companies promising quick fixes for high upfront fees.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What is overdraft protection?", "overdraft explained", "should i get overdraft on my chequing"], a: "Overdraft protection lets your chequing account go below zero up to a set limit, so payments don't bounce when you're short. It's a form of borrowing: banks usually charge interest on the overdrawn amount and sometimes a monthly or per-use fee. It can help avoid returned-payment fees, but relying on it regularly gets expensive. Check your bank's fees and consider a small emergency fund instead.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["How do I fix an error on my credit report?", "wrong info on equifax report", "dispute credit report transunion"], a: "Get your reports from both Equifax and TransUnion, since they can differ. If you find an error, like an account you don't recognize or a payment wrongly marked late, file a dispute directly with the credit bureau that shows it, usually online, and include any proof you have. You can also contact the lender that reported it. If the account isn't yours at all, it could be identity theft, so contact your bank and the police too.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Should my partner and I open a joint account?", "joint bank account pros and cons", "how do joint accounts work"], a: "A joint account is shared by two or more people, and each can usually deposit and withdraw. Some general considerations: it's convenient for shared bills, but each person can generally access all the money, and joint debts like overdrafts can affect both of you. Many couples keep a joint account for shared expenses and separate accounts for personal spending. Your choice depends on your relationship and goals; a financial advisor can help with personal advice.", lang: "en", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Are payday loans a good idea?", "payday loan canada", "need money fast payday lender"], a: "Payday loans are very expensive short-term loans, and the high costs can trap people in repeated borrowing. Before using one, consider alternatives: asking your bank about a small loan or overdraft, a payment arrangement with whoever you owe, help from community programs, or a non-profit credit counsellor. Provinces have rules for payday lenders, so check that any lender is licensed where you live. If debt is piling up, credit counselling can help.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["What is a GIC?", "gic meaning", "how do GICs work in canada"], a: "A GIC (Guaranteed Investment Certificate) is a deposit where you lend money to a bank or credit union for a set term, and they pay you interest. Your principal is generally guaranteed, and many GICs are eligible for deposit insurance within the limits. The trade-off is less flexibility: non-redeemable GICs lock your money until maturity. GICs can be held in a regular account, TFSA, RRSP, or FHSA. Rates vary by term and institution.", lang: "en", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["C'est quand la date limite pour faire mes impôts?", "date limite impots", "Jusqu'à quand j'ai pour produire ma déclaration de revenus?"], a: "Pour la plupart des gens, la date limite pour produire ta déclaration de revenus est le 30 avril de l'année suivant l'année d'imposition. Si tu as un solde à payer, il est aussi dû le 30 avril, sinon des intérêts s'appliquent. Au Québec, c'est la même date pour la déclaration fédérale (ARC) et la déclaration provinciale (Revenu Québec). Si le 30 avril tombe un week-end ou un jour férié, l'échéance est généralement reportée au jour ouvrable suivant.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Je suis travailleur autonome, j'ai jusqu'à quand pour faire mes impôts?", "travailleur autonome date limite", "Mon chum est à son compte, c'est quoi notre date limite?"], a: "Si toi ou ton conjoint exploitez une entreprise (travailleur autonome), la date limite pour produire vos déclarations est le 15 juin. Attention : tout solde à payer doit quand même être réglé au plus tard le 30 avril. Si tu paies après, l'ARC et Revenu Québec calculent des intérêts sur le montant impayé. Un bon réflexe : estime ce que tu dois et paie-le d'ici le 30 avril, puis produis tes déclarations avant le 15 juin.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Qu'est-ce qui arrive si je produis ma déclaration en retard?", "impots en retard penalite", "j'ai oublié de faire mes impôts, c'est grave?"], a: "Si tu as un solde à payer et que tu produis en retard, tu peux avoir une pénalité pour production tardive, en plus des intérêts sur le montant dû. Les pénalités peuvent être plus élevées si tu as déjà été en retard. Si tu attends un remboursement, il n'y a généralement pas de pénalité, mais tes prestations, comme le crédit pour la TPS/TVH ou l'Allocation canadienne pour enfants, peuvent être retardées. Le mieux : produis le plus vite possible, même si tu ne peux pas tout payer.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Pourquoi je dois faire deux déclarations d'impôt au Québec?", "deux rapports d'impots quebec", "c'est quoi le TP-1"], a: "Au Québec, tu produis une déclaration fédérale auprès de l'Agence du revenu du Canada (ARC) et une déclaration provinciale séparée, la TP-1, auprès de Revenu Québec. Ailleurs au Canada, l'impôt provincial est calculé sur la déclaration fédérale, mais le Québec gère son propre régime fiscal. Les deux ont les mêmes échéances. La plupart des logiciels certifiés préparent les deux en même temps.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un relevé 1?", "rl-1 vs t4", "j'ai reçu un T4 et un relevé 1, je fais quoi"], a: "Le relevé 1 (RL-1) est le feuillet québécois qui indique tes revenus d'emploi et les retenues sur ta paie. Il sert pour ta déclaration provinciale avec Revenu Québec. Le T4, lui, sert pour ta déclaration fédérale avec l'ARC. Si tu travailles au Québec, tu reçois habituellement les deux. Les montants peuvent être un peu différents d'un feuillet à l'autre, alors utilise chacun pour la bonne déclaration.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un avis de cotisation?", "avis de cotisation explication", "j'ai reçu mon avis de cotisation ça veut dire quoi"], a: "L'avis de cotisation est le document que l'ARC (ou Revenu Québec pour ta déclaration provinciale) t'envoie après avoir traité ta déclaration. Il indique si tu as droit à un remboursement, si tu dois un solde, et les changements apportés à ta déclaration. L'avis fédéral montre aussi ton plafond de déduction REER pour l'année suivante. Garde-le précieusement : les prêteurs le demandent souvent pour une hypothèque.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Mon dossier de l'ARC?", "mon dossier arc ça sert à quoi", "comment m'inscrire a mon dossier"], a: "Mon dossier est le portail en ligne sécurisé de l'ARC. Tu peux y :\n- voir tes avis de cotisation et tes feuillets\n- suivre ton remboursement ou ton solde\n- consulter ton plafond REER et tes droits CELI\n- voir tes versements de prestations\n- t'inscrire au dépôt direct et changer ton adresse\nPour t'inscrire, va sur canada.ca. Revenu Québec a aussi son propre service en ligne pour les citoyens.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment produire ma déclaration en ligne?", "c'est quoi impotnet", "faire ses impots sur internet"], a: "Tu peux utiliser un logiciel d'impôt certifié par l'ARC, puis transmettre ta déclaration fédérale par IMPÔTNET. Au Québec, les logiciels certifiés par Revenu Québec permettent aussi d'envoyer ta TP-1 en ligne. C'est plus rapide que le papier et tes remboursements arrivent plus vite, surtout avec le dépôt direct. Certains logiciels sont gratuits; la liste des produits certifiés est publiée sur canada.ca et sur le site de Revenu Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Est-ce qu'il y a des cliniques d'impôt gratuites?", "faire faire mes impots gratuitement", "comptoir d'impot benevole"], a: "Oui! Des organismes communautaires offrent des comptoirs d'impôt gratuits où des bénévoles remplissent les déclarations des personnes admissibles. Au Québec, le programme est offert conjointement par l'ARC et Revenu Québec, donc tes deux déclarations peuvent être faites. C'est pour les personnes à revenu modeste avec une situation fiscale simple. Les critères de revenu changent; vérifie-les sur canada.ca ou sur le site de Revenu Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment m'inscrire au dépôt direct?", "depot direct arc", "je veux recevoir mon remboursement dans mon compte"], a: "Tu peux t'inscrire au dépôt direct :\n- dans Mon dossier de l'ARC\n- par ton institution financière, en ligne, si elle offre l'inscription\n- par téléphone ou par formulaire\nRevenu Québec a son propre dépôt direct, que tu peux configurer dans ton dossier en ligne. Une fois inscrit, tes remboursements et prestations arrivent directement dans ton compte, plus vite et de façon plus sûre qu'un chèque.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment modifier ma déclaration après l'avoir envoyée?", "j'ai fait une erreur dans mes impots", "corriger ma declaration de revenus"], a: "Attends d'avoir reçu ton avis de cotisation, puis demande une modification. Pour le fédéral, utilise « Modifier ma déclaration » dans Mon dossier de l'ARC, ou la fonction ReTRANSMETTRE de ton logiciel certifié. Pour le Québec, tu peux faire une demande de modification auprès de Revenu Québec, en ligne ou par formulaire. N'envoie pas une deuxième déclaration complète, ça ralentit le traitement. Garde tes pièces justificatives.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi des acomptes provisionnels?", "acomptes provisionnels obligatoire?", "on me demande de payer de l'impot par trimestre"], a: "Les acomptes provisionnels sont des paiements d'impôt faits pendant l'année quand il n'y a pas assez d'impôt retenu à la source, par exemple si tu es travailleur autonome, propriétaire d'un immeuble locatif ou investisseur. Tu peux devoir en faire si ton impôt à payer dépasse un certain seuil; les seuils sont publiés sur canada.ca et par Revenu Québec. Les dates habituelles sont le 15 mars, 15 juin, 15 septembre et 15 décembre.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Je viens d'arriver au Canada, pourquoi je devrais faire mes impôts?", "nouvel arrivant impots", "immigrant premiere declaration de revenus"], a: "Produire ta déclaration te permet de recevoir des prestations auxquelles tu pourrais avoir droit, comme le crédit pour la TPS/TVH et l'Allocation canadienne pour enfants, ainsi que des prestations provinciales; au Québec, par exemple, le crédit d'impôt pour solidarité. Même avec peu ou pas de revenu canadien, ça vaut la peine. Tu indiques ta date d'arrivée sur ta déclaration. Les comptoirs d'impôt bénévoles accueillent les nouveaux arrivants dont la situation est simple.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Je me suis marié, je dois le dire à l'ARC?", "changement etat civil impots", "on est rendus conjoints de fait, je fais quoi"], a: "Oui. Si ton état civil change (mariage, union de fait, séparation, divorce ou veuvage), tu dois aviser l'ARC au plus tard à la fin du mois qui suit le changement, par exemple dans Mon dossier. Au Québec, avise aussi Revenu Québec. C'est important, car plusieurs prestations, comme l'Allocation canadienne pour enfants et le crédit pour la TPS/TVH, sont calculées selon le revenu familial.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un REER?", "reer explication", "à quoi sert un régime enregistré d'épargne-retraite"], a: "Le REER (régime enregistré d'épargne-retraite) est un compte pour épargner en vue de la retraite. Tes cotisations sont déductibles de ton revenu, ce qui peut réduire ton impôt. Tes placements croissent à l'abri de l'impôt, et tu paies de l'impôt au moment du retrait. Ton plafond de cotisation dépend de ton revenu gagné; il est indiqué sur ton avis de cotisation fédéral et dans Mon dossier. Le plafond annuel change chaque année.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quand la date limite pour cotiser au REER?", "date limite reer", "jusqu'a quand pour mettre dans mon reer"], a: "Tu peux déduire les cotisations faites pendant l'année, plus celles faites dans les 60 premiers jours de l'année suivante. En général, ça donne le 1er mars, ou le 29 février lors d'une année bissextile. Si la date tombe un week-end, elle peut être reportée; vérifie la date confirmée sur canada.ca. Les cotisations faites dans cette période peuvent être déduites pour l'année précédente ou l'année en cours.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un CELI?", "celi explication", "compte d'épargne libre d'impôt ça marche comment"], a: "Le CELI (compte d'épargne libre d'impôt) est un compte enregistré où tes revenus de placement sont libres d'impôt. Tes cotisations ne sont pas déductibles, mais les intérêts, dividendes et gains en capital gagnés dans le compte ne sont pas imposés, et les retraits sont libres d'impôt. Tu accumules des droits de cotisation à partir de 18 ans si tu résides au Canada et as un NAS. Le plafond annuel change; vérifie tes droits dans Mon dossier.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Si je retire de mon CELI, je peux le remettre tout de suite?", "retrait celi recotiser", "j'ai sorti de l'argent de mon celi, quand je récupère mes droits"], a: "Le montant que tu retires de ton CELI est ajouté à tes droits de cotisation le 1er janvier de l'année suivante, pas tout de suite. Donc si tu retires en juin et que tu remets l'argent en août de la même année sans avoir assez de droits inutilisés, tu pourrais faire une cotisation excédentaire. Un transfert direct entre tes propres CELI dans différentes institutions ne compte pas comme un retrait.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["J'ai trop cotisé dans mon CELI, qu'est-ce qui arrive?", "cotisation excedentaire celi", "depassé mon plafond celi"], a: "Si tu dépasses tes droits de cotisation CELI, tu dois généralement payer un impôt de pénalité chaque mois sur le montant excédentaire, tant qu'il reste dans le compte. L'ARC peut t'envoyer une lettre et te demander de produire une déclaration CELI. Pour arrêter la pénalité, retire l'excédent le plus rapidement possible. Tes droits sont dans Mon dossier, mais les transactions très récentes peuvent ne pas y figurer, alors garde tes propres registres.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le CELIAPP?", "celiapp explication", "compte pour acheter ma premiere maison"], a: "Le CELIAPP (compte d'épargne libre d'impôt pour l'achat d'une première propriété) aide les premiers acheteurs à épargner pour leur mise de fonds. Il combine des avantages du REER et du CELI : les cotisations sont généralement déductibles, et les retraits admissibles pour acheter une première maison sont libres d'impôt. Il y a des plafonds annuels et à vie, et le compte ne peut rester ouvert qu'un certain temps. Les règles à jour sont sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un REEE?", "reee et subventions", "est-ce que le gouvernement ajoute de l'argent dans le reee"], a: "Le REEE (régime enregistré d'épargne-études) sert à épargner pour les études postsecondaires d'un enfant. Le gros avantage : les subventions. Le fédéral ajoute la Subvention canadienne pour l'épargne-études (SCEE), calculée selon un pourcentage de tes cotisations, et les résidents du Québec peuvent aussi recevoir l'incitatif québécois à l'épargne-études (IQEE). Les familles à faible revenu peuvent recevoir des montants supplémentaires. L'enfant doit avoir un NAS. Les taux à jour sont sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'Allocation canadienne pour enfants?", "ACE comment ça marche", "allocation pour enfants federal"], a: "L'Allocation canadienne pour enfants (ACE) est un paiement mensuel non imposable versé aux familles admissibles pour les aider avec les coûts liés aux enfants de moins de 18 ans. Le montant dépend du revenu familial net, du nombre d'enfants et de leur âge, et il est recalculé chaque juillet. Toi et ton conjoint devez produire vos déclarations chaque année pour continuer à la recevoir. Au Québec, Retraite Québec verse aussi l'Allocation famille.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le crédit pour la TPS/TVH?", "credit tps comment l'avoir", "pourquoi j'ai reçu un paiement de TPS"], a: "Le crédit pour la TPS/TVH est un paiement trimestriel non imposable qui aide les personnes et familles à revenu faible ou modeste à compenser les taxes de vente qu'elles paient. Il n'y a généralement pas de demande séparée : l'ARC vérifie ton admissibilité quand tu produis ta déclaration. Les montants et seuils changent chaque année. Pour le recevoir, produis ta déclaration chaque année, même sans revenu.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le crédit d'impôt pour personnes handicapées?", "CIPH comment faire une demande", "credit d'impot handicap"], a: "Le crédit d'impôt pour personnes handicapées (CIPH) est un crédit non remboursable pour les personnes ayant une déficience grave et prolongée des fonctions physiques ou mentales, ou pour les proches qui les soutiennent. Pour le demander, tu remplis le formulaire de demande, et un professionnel de la santé certifie la partie médicale. Une fois approuvé, il peut donner accès à d'autres programmes comme le REEI. Méfie-toi des entreprises qui exigent des frais élevés : la demande est gratuite.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux déduire mes frais médicaux?", "frais medicaux impots", "lunettes et dentiste deductible?"], a: "Oui, par le crédit d'impôt pour frais médicaux. Tu peux demander les frais admissibles payés pour toi ou ta famille qui n'ont pas été remboursés par une assurance, comme des médicaments sur ordonnance, des soins dentaires ou des lunettes. Seule la partie au-dessus d'un seuil lié à ton revenu compte, et ce seuil change chaque année. Les familles regroupent souvent leurs frais sur une seule déclaration. Le Québec a aussi son propre crédit.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment fonctionne le crédit pour frais de scolarité?", "frais de scolarité impots", "T2202 je fais quoi avec"], a: "Ton collège ou ton université te remet un T2202 pour le fédéral et, au Québec, un relevé 8 pour Revenu Québec. Le crédit pour frais de scolarité est non remboursable : il réduit ton impôt à payer, mais ne crée pas de remboursement à lui seul. Si tu ne l'utilises pas au complet, tu peux reporter le montant inutilisé aux années suivantes, ou en transférer une partie à un parent ou grand-parent, jusqu'à une limite.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Je fais du télétravail, est-ce que je peux déduire mon bureau à la maison?", "teletravail deduction", "bureau a domicile employe impots"], a: "Peut-être, si tu remplis les conditions. En général, ton employeur doit t'avoir obligé à travailler de la maison, tu dois y avoir travaillé plus de 50 % du temps pendant au moins quatre semaines consécutives (ou utiliser l'espace seulement pour le travail et pour rencontrer des clients régulièrement), et ton employeur doit remplir le formulaire T2200 (au Québec, aussi le TP-64.3 pour Revenu Québec). La méthode à taux fixe temporaire de la pandémie n'existe plus.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je dois déclarer mes revenus d'à-côté?", "side hustle impots", "je vends sur etsy faut-tu que je le declare"], a: "Oui. Les revenus d'un travail d'appoint, comme la pige, la vente d'artisanat, le tutorat ou les applis de livraison, doivent être déclarés, même sans feuillet. Tu les déclares comme revenus d'entreprise et tu peux déduire les dépenses raisonnables pour les gagner. Tu pourrais aussi devoir t'inscrire aux fichiers de la TPS/TVH et de la TVQ. Les plateformes numériques transmettent maintenant certaines informations aux autorités fiscales. Un comptable (CPA) peut t'aider à partir du bon pied.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que la crypto est imposable?", "impot sur bitcoin", "je dois declarer mes gains crypto?"], a: "Oui. Quand tu vends, échanges, dépenses ou donnes de la crypto, c'est généralement une disposition, et tu dois déclarer ton gain ou ta perte. Selon ton activité, c'est traité comme un gain en capital ou comme un revenu d'entreprise. Échanger une crypto contre une autre compte aussi. Le simple fait d'acheter et de garder n'est pas imposé tant que tu ne disposes pas de ta crypto. Garde un registre de chaque transaction. Si c'est complexe, consulte un comptable (CPA).", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Quelqu'un de l'ARC m'appelle et dit que la police va venir m'arrêter, c'est vrai?", "arnaque arc", "l'agence du revenu veut des cartes cadeaux"], a: "C'est une arnaque. L'ARC ne menace jamais d'arrestation, de prison ou d'expulsion, et ne demande jamais de paiement en cartes-cadeaux, cartes prépayées ou cryptomonnaie. Raccroche et ne donne aucune information. Si tu veux vérifier, connecte-toi toi-même à Mon dossier ou appelle l'ARC au numéro sur canada.ca. Tu peux signaler l'arnaque au Centre antifraude du Canada. Si tu as déjà payé, contacte ta banque et la police.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["J'ai reçu un texto qui dit que l'ARC m'envoie un remboursement par virement, je clique?", "texto remboursement arc", "sms arc lien remboursement"], a: "Ne clique pas! Les textos qui annoncent un remboursement de l'ARC ou un virement Interac qui t'attend sont une arnaque courante pour voler tes informations bancaires. L'ARC n'envoie pas de remboursements par virement Interac et ne te demande pas tes renseignements par lien texto. Supprime le message. Pour vérifier un vrai remboursement, connecte-toi à Mon dossier. Si tu as entré des infos, appelle ta banque tout de suite.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Combien de temps je dois garder mes documents d'impôt?", "garder recus impots combien d'annees", "conserver feuillets combien de temps"], a: "En général, garde tes documents d'impôt et pièces justificatives pendant six ans après la fin de l'année d'imposition à laquelle ils se rapportent. Ça inclut tes feuillets (T4, relevés), reçus de dons, de frais médicaux, de garde d'enfants, de cotisations REER et tes avis de cotisation. Garde-les plus longtemps si tu as produit en retard ou si tu as une opposition en cours. Des copies numériques lisibles sont acceptées.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux autoriser quelqu'un à parler à l'ARC pour moi?", "autoriser un representant arc", "donner accès à mon comptable"], a: "Oui. Tu peux autoriser un représentant, comme un membre de ta famille ou ton comptable, à accéder à tes renseignements fiscaux et à faire affaire avec l'ARC pour toi. Tu peux le faire dans Mon dossier ou avec le formulaire d'autorisation. Tu choisis le niveau d'accès et tu peux l'annuler en tout temps. Pour Revenu Québec, il existe une procuration séparée.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment cacher mes revenus en argent comptant à l'impôt?", "pas declarer mon cash", "travail au noir comment pas me faire pogner"], a: "Je ne peux pas t'aider à cacher des revenus : ne pas les déclarer, c'est de l'évasion fiscale, et ça peut mener à des nouvelles cotisations, des pénalités, des intérêts et même des poursuites. La bonne voie est souvent moins pénible qu'on pense : déclare tes revenus et réclame toutes les dépenses et crédits auxquels tu as droit. Si tu as omis des revenus dans le passé, il existe des programmes de divulgation volontaire. Un comptable (CPA) peut t'aider.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Peux-tu me faire des faux reçus pour mes impôts?", "faux recus de dons", "modifier mon avis de cotisation pour la banque"], a: "Je ne peux pas t'aider avec ça. Fabriquer ou modifier des reçus, un T4 ou un avis de cotisation, c'est de la fraude, et les autorités fiscales et les prêteurs vérifient souvent. Par contre, je peux t'aider à trouver des déductions et crédits légitimes que tu oublies peut-être, comme les frais médicaux de toute la famille ou les frais de scolarité reportés. Un comptable (CPA) ou un comptoir d'impôt bénévole peut s'assurer que tu réclames tout ce à quoi tu as droit.", lang: "fr", topic: "tax" },
  { q: ["Comment ouvrir un compte de banque quand on arrive au Canada?", "ouvrir compte bancaire nouvel arrivant", "papiers pour ouvrir un compte"], a: "La plupart des banques et caisses ont des offres pour nouveaux arrivants. En général, il te faut :\n- une pièce d'identité valide, comme ton passeport\n- ton document d'immigration (carte de résident permanent, confirmation de résidence permanente, permis d'études ou de travail)\n- parfois une preuve d'adresse\nTu n'as pas toujours besoin d'un emploi pour ouvrir un compte de base. Compare les frais et prends rendez-vous.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi une cote de crédit?", "cote de credit canada", "comment fonctionne mon dossier de credit"], a: "La cote de crédit est un chiffre qui montre aux prêteurs si tu es susceptible de rembourser ce que tu empruntes. Au Canada, elle va généralement de 300 à 900; plus elle est haute, mieux c'est. Elle est calculée par les deux agences d'évaluation du crédit, Equifax et TransUnion, selon ton historique de paiements, ton utilisation du crédit, l'ancienneté de tes comptes et tes nouvelles demandes. Tu peux consulter ton dossier gratuitement auprès des deux.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Comment me bâtir un dossier de crédit?", "pas d'historique de credit au canada", "bâtir mon crédit nouvel arrivant"], a: "Quelques façons courantes :\n- obtenir une carte de crédit, par exemple une carte pour nouveaux arrivants ou une carte garantie\n- l'utiliser pour de petits achats et payer le solde au complet à temps chaque mois\n- garder ton solde bien en dessous de ta limite\n- payer toutes tes factures à temps\n- éviter de faire plusieurs demandes de crédit en même temps\nBâtir une bonne cote prend du temps, souvent des mois, voire des années.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la différence entre un compte chèques et un compte épargne?", "compte cheque vs epargne", "j'ai besoin de quel compte"], a: "Le compte chèques sert à ton argent de tous les jours : factures, paiements par carte de débit, virements et dépôts de paie. Il rapporte peu ou pas d'intérêts. Le compte épargne sert à mettre de l'argent de côté; il rapporte généralement plus d'intérêts, mais peut limiter ou facturer les retraits. Beaucoup de gens utilisent les deux : un pour les dépenses, l'autre pour leurs objectifs et leur fonds d'urgence.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Comment fonctionne le virement Interac?", "virement interac c'est quoi", "depot automatique interac"], a: "Le virement Interac te permet d'envoyer de l'argent de ton compte à quelqu'un avec son adresse courriel ou son numéro de cellulaire, par ton appli ou ton site bancaire. Si la personne a activé le dépôt automatique, l'argent va directement dans son compte, sans question de sécurité, ce qui est plus sûr. Sinon, elle reçoit un avis et répond à une question de sécurité. Une fois déposé, un virement ne peut généralement pas être annulé, alors vérifie bien le destinataire.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Quelles sont les arnaques par virement Interac?", "arnaque virement interac", "quelqu'un m'a envoyé trop d'argent et veut que je rembourse"], a: "Méfie-toi :\n- des faux avis de virement par courriel ou texto avec un lien pour voler tes identifiants bancaires\n- des arnaques au trop-payé, où quelqu'un t'envoie trop et te demande de rembourser la différence\n- des vendeurs en ligne qui veulent être payés avant que tu voies l'article\n- des faux propriétaires qui demandent un dépôt avant la visite\nAccepte les virements seulement dans ta vraie appli bancaire. Un virement, c'est comme de l'argent comptant.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que mon argent est assuré si ma banque fait faillite?", "assurance-depots quebec", "SADC ou AMF caisse desjardins"], a: "En général, oui, jusqu'à une certaine limite. Les dépôts admissibles dans les banques membres sont protégés par la Société d'assurance-dépôts du Canada (SADC). Au Québec, les dépôts dans les caisses Desjardins sont protégés par l'Autorité des marchés financiers (AMF). La protection couvre les dépôts comme les comptes chèques et épargne et certains CPG, mais pas les actions, les fonds communs ou la crypto. Vérifie les limites à jour auprès de ton institution.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la différence entre un taux fixe et un taux variable pour une hypothèque?", "hypotheque fixe ou variable explication", "c'est quoi le test de resistance"], a: "Avec un taux fixe, ton taux reste le même pendant le terme : tes paiements sont prévisibles. Avec un taux variable, le taux suit le taux préférentiel du prêteur, donc tes frais d'intérêt peuvent monter ou baisser. L'amortissement, c'est la durée totale pour rembourser le prêt. Le test de résistance t'oblige à prouver que tu pourrais payer à un taux plus élevé que ton taux réel. Un professionnel hypothécaire peut t'expliquer les options.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Marge de crédit ou carte de crédit, c'est quoi la différence?", "marge de credit explication", "marge vs carte"], a: "Les deux te permettent d'emprunter jusqu'à une limite. La marge de crédit a souvent un taux d'intérêt plus bas, mais l'intérêt commence dès que tu empruntes; on l'utilise souvent pour des dépenses plus grosses ou planifiées. La carte de crédit a un taux plus élevé, mais offre un délai de grâce sans intérêt sur les achats si tu paies le solde au complet avant l'échéance. Payer seulement le minimum coûte cher.", lang: "fr", topic: "banking" },
  { q: ["Comment faire un budget?", "faire un budget simple", "aide moi a faire mon budget"], a: "Une façon simple de commencer :\n1. Note ton revenu net mensuel.\n2. Suis tes dépenses pendant un mois.\n3. Sépare tes dépenses : besoins (loyer, épicerie, transport), envies, et épargne ou dettes.\n4. Fixe une limite pour chaque catégorie et ajuste au besoin.\n5. Automatise ton épargne le jour de la paie.\nApplication, chiffrier ou carnet, prends l'outil que tu vas vraiment utiliser.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un fonds d'urgence?", "fonds d'urgence combien", "j'ai-tu besoin d'un coussin financier"], a: "Un fonds d'urgence, c'est de l'argent mis de côté pour les imprévus, comme une perte d'emploi, une réparation d'auto ou un voyage urgent, pour éviter de t'endetter. Une règle courante : trois à six mois de dépenses essentielles, mais même un petit montant aide. Garde-le dans un endroit facile d'accès et séparé de ton argent de tous les jours, comme un compte épargne. Commence petit et ajoute automatiquement à chaque paie.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je devrais acheter du bitcoin?", "crypto bon placement?", "je mets mes economies dans la crypto?"], a: "Quelques points généraux : le prix des cryptos peut varier énormément, elles ne sont pas couvertes par l'assurance-dépôts, des plateformes peuvent faire faillite ou être piratées, et les arnaques sont fréquentes. Les gains sont aussi imposables. Je ne peux pas te donner de conseils financiers personnels ni te dire d'acheter un actif. Un conseiller financier autorisé ou un planificateur financier peut regarder tes objectifs et ta tolérance au risque.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["CELI ou REER, lequel est mieux pour moi?", "je devrais mettre mon argent dans un celi ou un reer", "reer ou celi en premier"], a: "Quelques considérations générales : le REER donne une déduction maintenant et tu paies l'impôt au retrait, donc il aide souvent plus quand ton revenu actuel est plus élevé qu'à la retraite. Le CELI ne donne pas de déduction, mais les retraits sont libres d'impôt et flexibles. Plusieurs personnes utilisent les deux. Je ne peux pas te donner de conseils financiers personnels. Consulte un conseiller financier autorisé ou un planificateur financier, et un comptable (CPA) pour l'aspect fiscal.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je devrais rembourser mes dettes ou investir?", "payer mes dettes ou placer mon argent", "rembourser ma carte ou mettre dans mon celi"], a: "Quelques considérations générales : les dettes à intérêt élevé, comme les cartes de crédit, coûtent souvent plus cher que ce que les placements rapportent de façon fiable, donc beaucoup de gens les remboursent en priorité. Pour les dettes à faible taux, c'est moins clair et ça dépend de tes objectifs, de ton confort avec le risque et de ton fonds d'urgence. Je ne peux pas te donner de conseils financiers personnels. Un conseiller financier autorisé ou un planificateur financier peut t'aider.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
  { q: ["Quelle action va monter?", "donne moi une action a acheter", "dans quoi je devrais investir"], a: "Je ne peux pas prédire le prix des actions ni te recommander des placements précis, et personne ne peut savoir de façon fiable quelle action va monter. En général, on tient compte de ses objectifs, de son horizon de placement, de sa tolérance au risque, de la diversification et des frais. Vérifie aussi que toute personne qui te vend des placements est inscrite auprès de l'AMF ou d'un autre organisme de réglementation. Pour un plan personnalisé, consulte un conseiller financier autorisé.", lang: "fr", topic: "finance" },
];
